post_install() {
  # Profiles ship in COMPLAIN mode. Enforcing a profile that has never seen the
  # program run on this machine's hardware will deny something it needs — a
  # graphics path, a font directory, a codec — and a browser that will not start
  # is how people learn to disable AppArmor entirely.
  #
  # `steel-profile status` names anything left in complain mode, and steel-check
  # reports it as a warning, so this is a starting state rather than a resting
  # one.
  if command -v aa-complain >/dev/null; then
    for profile in /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.firefox \
                   /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.steel-shell \
                   /etc/apparmor.d/usr.lib.steelos.captive-portal-helper; do
      [ -f "$profile" ] && aa-complain "$profile" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    done
  fi

  cat <<'MSG'

steel-apparmor: two things to do.

1. AppArmor is NOT active until the kernel is told to enable it.

   Arch builds AppArmor into the kernel but does not select it, so
   installing the package alone changes nothing. Add this to your kernel
   command line and reboot:

       lsm=landlock,lockdown,yama,integrity,apparmor apparmor=1 security=apparmor

   The full fragment is at /usr/share/steel-apparmor/cmdline. The lsm= list
   must be complete — naming only apparmor silently disables landlock,
   lockdown, yama, and integrity.

   On an image deployment this is baked into the signed UKI and you do not
   have to do anything.

       systemctl enable --now apparmor.service

2. The shipped profiles are in COMPLAIN mode.

   Complain logs violations and permits them, so nothing is confined yet.
   Use each program normally for a few days, then:

       steel-profile refine /usr/lib/firefox/firefox
       steel-profile enforce /usr/lib/firefox/firefox

   `steel-profile status` lists anything still in complain mode, and
   `steel-check sandbox.apparmor-enforcing` reports it as a warning.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  # Reload rather than restart: restarting drops all profiles briefly, and a
  # window with no confinement during an unattended update is worth avoiding.
  if systemctl is-active --quiet apparmor.service; then
    systemctl reload apparmor.service || true
  fi
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'MSG'

steel-apparmor: removing this package leaves the profiles in /etc/apparmor.d.

  They are marked as config files, so pacman keeps your edits. To remove
  them and stop confining these programs:

      rm /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.firefox
      rm /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.steel-shell
      rm /etc/apparmor.d/usr.lib.steelos.captive-portal-helper
      systemctl reload apparmor.service

MSG
}
